Ladybird derives a number input’s preferred character count from its
finite min/max bounds and authored step precision. It counts a leading
minus sign, integer digits with leading zeros trimmed (at least one),
and all fractional digits as written. A decimal point reserves a column
when there are fractional digits. Exponent forms in any of these
attributes retain the default width, without expanding the exponent.
A valid explicit size takes priority over this calculation.

Blink measures the columns in NumberInputType::GetSizeWithDecoration,
using CalculateRenderSize. WebKit calls calculateRenderSize from
NumberInputType::sizeShouldIncludeDecoration. Both derive their sizes
from decimal bounds and step, including exponent forms. Gecko uses
Decimal in HTMLInputElement::GetNumberInputCols, while GetCols gives
an explicit size priority over the derived character count.

Ladybird intentionally counts authored decimal text without Decimal
normalization or its precision limits, and falls back to the default
width for exponent forms. That keeps the common bounded-decimal case
simple, but differs from the other engines for exponent forms. A zero
takes one column however it’s written, as in all three engines.

The styled spin buttons reserve their own inline size beside the text,
as Blink and WebKit do through their decoration-sizing paths, and Gecko
does in nsTextControlFrame::CalcFixedSize. Explicit CSS dimensions and
field-sizing retain their normal sizing behavior. The explicit-size
priority agrees with Gecko and the imported tentative WPT; Chrome
ignores size on number inputs. Ladybird retains the authored precision
of a negative step, while Gecko uses the normalized GetStep result.
